  "findings": [
    {
      "id": "duration-boundaries",
      "text": "Sleep with d <= 0 is documented to return immediately. For positive d, it pauses the calling goroutine for at least d. Sleep has no body in the excerpt. NewTimer and AfterFunc, and Reset on an initialized timer, instead pass a deadline computed by when to the runtime. For d <= 0, that deadline is the sampled runtimeNano(). For positive d, it is runtimeNano() + int64(d), with a negative overflow result replaced by MaxInt64. A deadline of now does not establish that a send or callback has occurred by the time the public call returns.",
      "citation": {
        "line": 25,
        "fragment": "if d <= 0 {"
      },
      "question": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "channel-creation-and-delivery",
      "text": "NewTimer creates a fresh channel, schedules a one-shot sendTime through newTimer, assigns that channel to t.C, and returns t. After takes the same path and returns only that call's t.C. When invoked, sendTime attempts a nonblocking send of Now() minus the runtime-supplied delay, and takes the default branch if the send cannot proceed. The source says the runtime can defer invoking sendTime until a receiver tries to receive, so reaching the deadline and receiving the value are distinct events. The Go 1.23 documentation also says an unreferenced timer can be collected before it fires without a Stop call.",
      "citation": {
        "line": 111,
        "fragment": "c := make(chan Time, 1)"
      },
      "question": "OPEN: Which runtime behavior makes the channel observably synchronous with capacity zero as documented in Go 1.23, given the capacity-one allocation in this excerpt?"
    },
    {
      "id": "channel-stop",
      "text": "On an initialized, running NewTimer timer with no receive yet, Stop delegates to stopTimer and is documented to return true under Go 1.23 semantics. If the timer has already expired or been stopped, Stop returns false, so a second Stop after a successful first Stop and no intervening Reset returns false without requiring any delivery. The wrapper passes the original timer and leaves t.C in place. Under the Go 1.23 contract, a receive begun after Stop returns cannot obtain a pre-Stop value and, absent a later Reset, blocks. Under the earlier buffered behavior a pending value could survive Stop. Calling Stop on a zero-value Timer panics before delegation.",
      "citation": {
        "line": 89,
        "fragment": "return stopTimer(t)"
      },
      "question": "OPEN: The pre-Go 1.23 advice says to drain when Stop returns false. Should it explicitly exclude histories where an earlier Stop succeeded or the value was already received, since those histories can leave a blocking drain?"
    },
    {
      "id": "channel-reset",
      "text": "Reset on a zero-value Timer panics before computing a deadline. On a NewTimer timer, Reset passes the same timer pointer, the new deadline, and a zero period to resetTimer. The channel stays the same. Reset returns true if the timer was active, including the documented Go 1.23 case where it is running and no value has been received. It returns false if the timer had expired or been stopped, and still schedules the new expiration. Thus a Reset following a successful Stop returns false without implying any earlier receive, and a subsequent Reset while the new setting is still active can return true. Under the Go 1.23 contract, a receive begun after Reset returns cannot get a value from the prior setting. Earlier buffered behavior could expose a stale value, so the documentation calls for stopping and draining first.",
      "citation": {
        "line": 143,
        "fragment": "return resetTimer(t, w, 0)"
      },
      "question": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "callback-stop",
      "text": "AfterFunc returns a one-shot timer with nil C and registers goFunc to launch f in its own goroutine when the runtime invokes it. If Stop wins while the timer is pending, it returns true and cancels that invocation. If the timer has expired, Stop can return false while the goroutine's call to f is still outstanding. Stop does not wait for completion. If the first Stop succeeded and no Reset intervenes, a second Stop also returns false, even though the canceled invocation never ran.",
      "citation": {
        "line": 71,
        "fragment": "if t.Stop returns false, then the timer has already expired"
      },
      "question": "OPEN: Should the AfterFunc-specific statement that false means f has started be limited to the expiration case, given that the general Stop contract also returns false after a successful Stop?"
    },
    {
      "id": "callback-reset",
      "text": "Reset on a pending AfterFunc timer reschedules the callback on the same timer and returns true. If the timer was stopped or had expired, Reset returns false and schedules a callback at the new deadline on that same timer, with C still nil. After a successful Stop this can be f's first invocation. After expiration the prior goroutine can still run or complete after Reset returns, and the newly scheduled invocation can overlap it. Reset's return value reports the prior timer state and provides no completion handshake.",
      "citation": {
        "line": 124,
        "fragment": "When Reset returns false, Reset neither waits for the prior f to"
      },
      "question": ""
    }
  ]
